    <article class="docs-prose">
      <h1 id="stage-05-inline-and-block-elements" class="group scroll-mt-20">Stage 05: Inline and block elements<a href="#stage-05-inline-and-block-elements" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Create <em>What is digital audio?</em> as a concept and <em>Trimming audio</em> as a task. Then add markup for control names, terms, and file names in the stage 04 topics. These inline elements identify the text's meaning for publishing, search, and translation tools.</p>
      <p>Add notes, lists, footnotes, simple tables, and code blocks to structure the topic bodies.</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> about 30 minutes. <strong>You need:</strong> stage 04 complete.</p>
      <p>Recorded diagnostic examples below come from earlier runs. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. Run the gate for your current checkout.</p>
      <h2 id="step-1-write-the-concept" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 1: Write the concept<a href="#step-1-write-the-concept" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita</code></strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE concept PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Concept//EN\" \"concept.dtd\">\n\n\u003cconcept id=\"what-is-digital-audio\">\n  \u003ctitle>What is digital audio?\u003c/title>\n  \u003cshortdesc>Digital audio is sound stored as numbers: samples taken thousands of times a second at a chosen precision.\u003c/shortdesc>\n  \u003cconbody>\n    \u003cp>Digital audio is sound that has been converted into a numerical representation.\n    A few key concepts help you make better recordings and edits.\u003c/p>\n    \u003csection>\n      \u003ctitle>Waveforms\u003c/title>\n      \u003cp>Sound travels through air as a continuous \u003cterm>waveform\u003c/term> of pressure changes.\n      A microphone converts these pressure changes into an electrical signal.\n      To store the signal digitally, the computer takes thousands of measurements, called \u003cterm>samples\u003c/term>, of the signal's amplitude every second.\u003c/p>\n    \u003c/section>\n    \u003csection>\n      \u003ctitle>Sample rate\u003c/title>\n      \u003cp>The \u003cterm>sample rate\u003c/term> is the number of samples captured per second, measured in hertz (Hz).\n      Common sample rates are:\u003c/p>\n      \u003csl>\n        \u003csli>44,100 Hz: CD quality, enough for most audio work\u003c/sli>\n        \u003csli>48,000 Hz: the standard for video production\u003c/sli>\n        \u003csli>96,000 Hz: used in professional music production\u003c/sli>\n      \u003c/sl>\n      \u003cp>A higher sample rate captures more detail but produces larger files.\u003cfn>File size grows in proportion to the sample rate: a 96 kHz file is more than twice the size of the same recording at 44.1 kHz.\u003c/fn>\u003c/p>\n    \u003c/section>\n    \u003csection>\n      \u003ctitle>Bit depth\u003c/title>\n      \u003cp>\u003cterm>Bit depth\u003c/term> determines how precisely each sample's amplitude is recorded.\u003c/p>\n      \u003csimpletable>\n        \u003csthead>\n          \u003cstentry>Bit depth\u003c/stentry>\n          \u003cstentry>Amplitude values\u003c/stentry>\n          \u003cstentry>Typical use\u003c/stentry>\n        \u003c/sthead>\n        \u003cstrow>\n          \u003cstentry>16-bit\u003c/stentry>\n          \u003cstentry>65,536\u003c/stentry>\n          \u003cstentry>CD audio\u003c/stentry>\n        \u003c/strow>\n        \u003cstrow>\n          \u003cstentry>24-bit\u003c/stentry>\n          \u003cstentry>over 16 million\u003c/stentry>\n          \u003cstentry>professional recording\u003c/stentry>\n        \u003c/strow>\n        \u003cstrow>\n          \u003cstentry>32-bit float\u003c/stentry>\n          \u003cstentry>effectively unlimited\u003c/stentry>\n          \u003cstentry>editing inside Audacity\u003c/stentry>\n        \u003c/strow>\n      \u003c/simpletable>\n    \u003c/section>\n    \u003csection>\n      \u003ctitle>Key audio terms\u003c/title>\n      \u003cdl>\n        \u003cdlentry>\n          \u003cdt>Amplitude\u003c/dt>\n          \u003cdd>The height of a sound wave, corresponding to how loud the sound is.\n          Measured in decibels (dB).\u003c/dd>\n        \u003c/dlentry>\n        \u003cdlentry>\n          \u003cdt>Clipping\u003c/dt>\n          \u003cdd>Distortion that occurs when the signal exceeds the maximum amplitude the system can represent.\n          It appears as flat-topped waveforms.\u003c/dd>\n        \u003c/dlentry>\n        \u003cdlentry>\n          \u003cdt>Mono and stereo\u003c/dt>\n          \u003cdd>Mono audio uses a single channel.\n          Stereo uses two channels, left and right, to create a sense of width.\u003c/dd>\n        \u003c/dlentry>\n      \u003c/dl>\n      \u003clq reftitle=\"The Audacity Manual\">Audio is measured in decibels because the ear hears ratios, not differences.\u003c/lq>\n    \u003c/section>\n  \u003c/conbody>\n\u003c/concept>"} lang="xml" title="topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the inline elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;term&gt;</code> marks a word at the point where it is being defined or used as a defined term. Stage 12 links terms to glossary entries; marking them now is what makes that possible.</li>
      <li>Choose markup that describes the text. For example, use <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> for a control label and leave a measurement such as "44,100 Hz" as text.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the block elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;sl&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;sli&gt;</code> is a simple list: short items, one line each, no bullets in most outputs. Use <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> when items are sentences.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;fn&gt;</code> is a footnote. It sits inline where the reference goes, and output moves the text to the foot of the page and leaves a marker.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;simpletable&gt;</code> uses <code>&lt;sthead&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;strow&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;stentry&gt;</code> for a regular grid. It supports column headers, <code>@keycol</code> for row headers, and <code>@relcolwidth</code> for relative widths, such as <code>relcolwidth="1* 2*"</code>. Use CALS <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> when you need spanning or a table title. See the <a href="https://docs.oasis-open.org/dita/dita/v1.3/os/part2-tech-content/langRef/attributes/simpletableAttributes.html">simple-table attributes</a>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;dl&gt;</code> is a definition list: each <code>&lt;dlentry&gt;</code> pairs a <code>&lt;dt&gt;</code> (the term) with a <code>&lt;dd&gt;</code> (its definition). It is the element for a list of named things with descriptions, which a <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> of "Name: description" items would leave unmarked.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;lq&gt;</code> is a long quotation, set off as a block. <code>@reftitle</code> names the source.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-2-write-the-task" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: Write the task<a href="#step-2-write-the-task" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>topics/trimming-audio.dita</code></strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE task PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN\" \"task.dtd\">\n\n\u003ctask id=\"trimming-audio\">\n  \u003ctitle>Trimming audio\u003c/title>\n  \u003cshortdesc>Remove silence, false starts and mistakes by selecting a region of the waveform and deleting it.\u003c/shortdesc>\n  \u003ctaskbody>\n    \u003ccontext>\n      \u003cp>Trimming removes unwanted audio from the start, end or middle of a track.\n      When you delete a region, the audio on either side closes the gap.\u003c/p>\n      \u003cnote type=\"tip\">Zoom in with \u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+1\u003c/uicontrol> to make precise selections; zoom back out with \u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+3\u003c/uicontrol>.\u003c/note>\n    \u003c/context>\n    \u003csteps>\n      \u003cstep>\n        \u003ccmd>Click and drag in the waveform to select the region you want to remove.\u003c/cmd>\n        \u003cinfo>\n          \u003cp>The selected region is highlighted.\n          To select the whole track, press \u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+A\u003c/uicontrol> (Windows and Linux) or \u003cuicontrol>Cmd+A\u003c/uicontrol> (macOS).\u003c/p>\n        \u003c/info>\n      \u003c/step>\n      \u003cstep>\n        \u003ccmd>Press \u003cuicontrol>Delete\u003c/uicontrol>, or choose \u003cmenucascade>\u003cuicontrol>Edit\u003c/uicontrol>\u003cuicontrol>\u003cshortcut>D\u003c/shortcut>elete\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/menucascade>.\u003c/cmd>\n        \u003cinfo>\n          \u003cnote type=\"important\">Deleting changes the audio in the project.\n          Undo with \u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+Z\u003c/uicontrol> if you removed the wrong region.\u003c/note>\n        \u003c/info>\n      \u003c/step>\n      \u003cstep>\n        \u003ccmd>To keep only the selection and discard everything else, choose \u003cmenucascade>\u003cuicontrol>Edit\u003c/uicontrol>\u003cuicontrol>Remove Special\u003c/uicontrol>\u003cuicontrol>Trim Audio\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/menucascade> instead.\u003c/cmd>\n      \u003c/step>\n      \u003cstep>\n        \u003ccmd>Play the track from just before the edit to check the join sounds natural.\u003c/cmd>\n        \u003cinfo>\n          \u003cp>If you hear a click at the join, select a few milliseconds around it and apply \u003cmenucascade>\u003cuicontrol>Effect\u003c/uicontrol>\u003cuicontrol>Fade In\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/menucascade> or \u003cuicontrol>Fade Out\u003c/uicontrol>.\u003c/p>\n        \u003c/info>\n      \u003c/step>\n      \u003cstep>\n        \u003ccmd>Save the project with \u003cmenucascade>\u003cuicontrol>File\u003c/uicontrol>\u003cuicontrol>Save Project\u003c/uicontrol>\u003cuicontrol>Save Project\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/menucascade>.\u003c/cmd>\n        \u003cinfo>\n          \u003cp>Audacity saves the project as a single \u003cfilepath>.aup3\u003c/filepath> file in the folder you choose; the \u003cwintitle>Save Project\u003c/wintitle> dialog suggests your Documents folder.\u003c/p>\n        \u003c/info>\n      \u003c/step>\n    \u003c/steps>\n    \u003cresult>\n      \u003cp>The unwanted audio is gone and the track plays through without a gap.\u003c/p>\n    \u003c/result>\n  \u003c/taskbody>\n\u003c/task>"} lang="xml" title="topics/trimming-audio.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the user-interface elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> is the name of a button, menu item, field or key as it appears in the interface: <code>Delete</code>, <code>Ctrl+A</code>, <code>Edit</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;menucascade&gt;</code> is a path through menus. Its children are <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> elements in order, and output joins them with an arrow: <strong>Edit &gt; Remove Special &gt; Trim Audio</strong>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;shortcut&gt;</code> marks the accelerator letter inside a control name, the underlined letter in a menu: <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;&lt;shortcut&gt;D&lt;/shortcut&gt;elete&lt;/uicontrol&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;wintitle&gt;</code> is the title of a window or dialog: the <em>Save Project</em> dialog.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;filepath&gt;</code> is a file name, path or extension: <code>.aup3</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the notes</strong>
      <ul><li><code>&lt;note&gt;</code> is a block that stands out from the text. <code>@type</code> says why: <code>tip</code> for a shortcut or a better way, <code>important</code> for something the reader must not skip, <code>warning</code> when something can go wrong, <code>caution</code> for a milder version, <code>note</code> (the default) for anything else. Stage 20 adds <code>trouble</code>. A note may sit in <code>&lt;context&gt;</code> or, inside a step, in <code>&lt;info&gt;</code>; it may not be a direct child of <code>&lt;step&gt;</code>.</li></ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p><code>&lt;shortcut&gt;</code> is only valid inside <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> in DITA 1.3. It marks the accelerator letter of a control, not a key combination. Key combinations such as <code>Ctrl+A</code> are plain <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code> text. If you write <code>&lt;cmd&gt;Press &lt;shortcut&gt;Delete&lt;/shortcut&gt;&lt;/cmd&gt;</code>, the gate reports:</p>
          <CodeBlock code={"topics/trimming-audio.dita:\n  22:170  error: The content of element type \"cmd\" does not match its content model.\n5 file(s): 4 valid, 1 invalid.\nFAIL: validation errors"} lang="plaintext" />
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <h2 id="step-3-mark-up-the-stage-04-topics" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 3: Mark up the stage 04 topics<a href="#step-3-mark-up-the-stage-04-topics" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><strong><code>topics/recording-your-first-track.dita</code></strong> The control names become <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code>, a tip goes into the context, and the sentence about the flat line becomes a <code>&lt;note type="warning"&gt;</code> inside the step's <code>&lt;info&gt;</code>.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n+++ b/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@\n     \u003ccontext>\n       \u003cp>This task walks you through making your first audio recording.\n       You need a microphone connected to your computer.\u003c/p>\n+      \u003cnote type=\"tip\">For best results, record in a quiet room and position the microphone 15 to 30 cm from your mouth.\u003c/note>\n     \u003c/context>\n     \u003csteps>\n       \u003cstep>\n@@ -17,27 +18,28 @@\n         \u003c/info>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep>\n-        \u003ccmd>Select your microphone from the recording device list in the Device Toolbar.\u003c/cmd>\n+        \u003ccmd>Select your microphone from the recording device list in the \u003cuicontrol>Device Toolbar\u003c/uicontrol>.\u003c/cmd>\n         \u003cinfo>\n-          \u003cp>The Device Toolbar is near the top of the window.\n+          \u003cp>The \u003cuicontrol>Device Toolbar\u003c/uicontrol> is near the top of the window.\n           The recording device list has a microphone icon next to it.\u003c/p>\n         \u003c/info>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep>\n-        \u003ccmd>Click Record to start recording.\u003c/cmd>\n+        \u003ccmd>Click \u003cuicontrol>Record\u003c/uicontrol>, or press \u003cuicontrol>R\u003c/uicontrol>, to start recording.\u003c/cmd>\n         \u003cinfo>\n-          \u003cp>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone.\n-          A flat line means the microphone is not selected correctly: click Stop and check the recording device.\u003c/p>\n+          \u003cp>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone.\u003c/p>\n+          \u003cnote type=\"warning\">A flat line instead of a waveform means the microphone is not selected correctly.\n+          Click \u003cuicontrol>Stop\u003c/uicontrol> and check the recording device.\u003c/note>\n         \u003c/info>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep>\n         \u003ccmd>Speak into your microphone, or play the audio you want to capture.\u003c/cmd>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep>\n-        \u003ccmd>Click Stop when you are finished.\u003c/cmd>\n+        \u003ccmd>Click \u003cuicontrol>Stop\u003c/uicontrol> when you are finished.\u003c/cmd>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep>\n-        \u003ccmd>Click Play to listen to your recording.\u003c/cmd>\n+        \u003ccmd>Click \u003cuicontrol>Play\u003c/uicontrol> to listen to your recording.\u003c/cmd>\n       \u003c/step>\n     \u003c/steps>\n     \u003cresult>"} lang="diff" title="topics/recording-your-first-track.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><strong><code>topics/supported-audio-formats.dita</code></strong> The reference gains a second section with an ordered list, a command name and a code block, and the terms and file names are marked.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n+++ b/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n@@ -7,7 +7,19 @@\n   \u003crefbody>\n     \u003csection>\n       \u003cp>Audacity imports and exports audio in several formats.\n-      Lossless formats keep every sample of the source; lossy formats discard detail to make smaller files.\u003c/p>\n+      \u003cterm>Lossless\u003c/term> formats keep every sample of the source; \u003cterm>lossy\u003c/term> formats discard detail to make smaller files.\n+      Exported files are named after the project, so \u003cfilepath>podcast-episode-1.aup3\u003c/filepath> exports as \u003cfilepath>podcast-episode-1.mp3\u003c/filepath>.\u003c/p>\n+    \u003c/section>\n+    \u003csection>\n+      \u003ctitle>Choosing a format\u003c/title>\n+      \u003cp>Keep a lossless master and export a lossy copy for distribution:\u003c/p>\n+      \u003col>\n+        \u003cli>Save the project (\u003cfilepath>.aup3\u003c/filepath>) while you are still editing.\u003c/li>\n+        \u003cli>Export a WAV or FLAC file as the archive copy.\u003c/li>\n+        \u003cli>Export an MP3 or OGG file to share.\u003c/li>\n+      \u003c/ol>\n+      \u003cp>On Linux you can convert between formats afterwards with \u003ccmdname>ffmpeg\u003c/cmdname>:\u003c/p>\n+      \u003ccodeblock>ffmpeg -i episode.wav -b:a 128k episode.mp3\u003c/codeblock>\n     \u003c/section>\n     \u003ctable>\n       \u003ctitle>Common audio formats\u003c/title>\n@@ -37,6 +49,7 @@\n             \u003centry>Compressed\u003c/entry>\n             \u003centry>Lossy\u003c/entry>\n             \u003centry>Widely supported, small files.\n+            Needs the \u003ci>LAME\u003c/i> encoder, bundled since version 2.3.2.\n             Good for sharing and the web.\u003c/entry>\n           \u003c/row>\n           \u003crow>"} lang="diff" title="topics/supported-audio-formats.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the new elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> is an ordered list. In a concept or reference it is for a sequence that is not a procedure; a procedure belongs in a task's <code>&lt;steps&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;cmdname&gt;</code> is the name of a command-line program. <code>&lt;codeph&gt;</code> (in the next stage) is a fragment of code inline; <code>&lt;codeblock&gt;</code> is a block of code, lines and spaces preserved. <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> is the same for preformatted text that is not code.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;i&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;b&gt;</code> exist, and <em>LAME</em> uses one because the encoder's name is conventionally set in italics. That is the case for them: typographic convention with no semantic element. When there is a semantic element, <code>&lt;term&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;cmdname&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;filepath&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ph&gt;</code>, use it instead; <code>&lt;b&gt;</code> says nothing a stylesheet or a translator can use.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;ph&gt;</code> is a phrase with no more specific meaning. It carries attributes (conditions in stage 14, ids for reuse in stage 11) when you need a handle on a run of text and none of the other inline elements fits.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-4-update-the-readme-and-run-the-gate" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 4: Update the README and run the gate<a href="#step-4-update-the-readme-and-run-the-gate" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Change the "You are on" line</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n \n Build a guide through 27 stages. Maps begin at stage 02; HTML publication begins at stage 03.\n \n-You are on stage 04: task and reference.\n+You are on stage 05: inline and block.\n \n ## Stages\n "} lang="diff" title="README.md" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Format and check</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita\nscripts/check-stage.sh"} lang="bash" />
      <CodeBlock code={"== validate-project  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\n5 file(s): 5 valid, 0 invalid.\n…\nSTAGE OK"} lang="plaintext" />
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="publish-your-changes" class="group scroll-mt-20">Publish your changes<a href="#publish-your-changes" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Add the lesson's topics to <code>audacity-guide.ditamap</code>, then rebuild the guide. The complete map at this checkpoint is:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n\u003cmap>\n  \u003ctitle>Audio user guide\u003c/title>\n  \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n  \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n  \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/trimming-audio.dita\"/>\n  \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n  \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"/>\n\u003c/map>"} lang="xml" title="audacity-guide.ditamap" />
      <CodeBlock code={"dita --project=project.json --output=out\npython3 scripts/check-output-links.py out"} lang="bash" />
      <h2 id="what-you-learned" class="group scroll-mt-20">What you learned<a href="#what-you-learned" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <ul>
      <li>Inline elements name what text is: <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;menucascade&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;shortcut&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;wintitle&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;filepath&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;term&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;cmdname&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ph&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;b&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;i&gt;</code> are for typographic convention only; prefer a semantic element.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;note&gt;</code> has types, and lives in <code>&lt;context&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;info&gt;</code>, not directly in a step.</li>
      <li>Lists: <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;sl&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;dl&gt;</code>; each is a different kind of list.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;simpletable&gt;</code> for short values, CALS <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> for everything else.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;fn&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;lq&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;codeblock&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> for footnotes, quotations and verbatim text.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;shortcut&gt;</code> only inside <code>&lt;uicontrol&gt;</code>.</li>
      </ul>
